{{also|Grenade|grénade|Grenåde|grenadé}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵerh₂-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|grenade}}, from {{der|en|fro|grenate}} in the phrase {{m|fro|pomme grenate||pomegranate}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ML.|pomum||apple}} + {{m|la|granatum||having grains}}. The ''-d'' developed in French under influence of {{cog|es|granada}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈneɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-grenade.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪd|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A small [[explosive]] [[device]], designed to be [[throw]]n by [[hand]] or [[launch]]ed using a [[rifle]], [[grenade launcher]], or [[rocket]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[pomegranate]].
# {{lb|en|heraldiccharge}} A [[charge]] similar to a [[fireball]], and made of a [[disc]]-shaped [[bomb]] [[shell]], but with only [[one]] set of [[flames]] at the [[top]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} An [[unattractive]] [[girl]].

